32	Your First Words on Screen	Write a program that prints exactly this line (nothing more, nothing less):\n\nHello, world!\n\nThere is no input. Do not print prompts. Spelling, capitalization, comma, space, and exclamation mark must match exactly. A trailing newline is fine.\n\nThis is the classic first program — it proves you can run code and send text to the output the judge reads.\n\nAllowed languages: Python, Java, or C++.	EASY	[{"input": "(none)", "output": "Hello, world!"}]	["No input", "Output must be exactly: Hello, world!", "No extra prompts or spaces"]	[{"stdin": "", "stdout": "Hello, world!"}]	2026-07-27 10:45:33.471923	["Start Here"]
33	The Number Mirror	Read one integer from the input and print that same integer back out.\n\nThe input is a single integer on one line. Print only that number — no labels, words, or questions. Negative numbers and zero are valid.\n\nSteps: read the value from standard input, then print it to standard output.\n\nExample\nInput: 42\nOutput: 42	EASY	[{"input": "7", "output": "7"}, {"input": "-3", "output": "-3"}, {"input": "0", "output": "0"}]	["-1000 <= n <= 1000", "Print only the integer", "No prompt text"]	[{"stdin": "7", "stdout": "7"}, {"stdin": "-3", "stdout": "-3"}, {"stdin": "0", "stdout": "0"}, {"stdin": "1000", "stdout": "1000"}]	2026-07-27 10:45:33.471923	["Start Here"]
34	Label the Box	Read one integer from input, store it in a variable, then print the value of that variable.\n\nUse a clearly named variable (for example: n, value, or number). Assign the input to it, then print the variable — not a hard-coded number. Print only the number.\n\nA variable is a named box that holds a value. Put the input in the box, then print what is in the box.\n\nExample\nInput: 15\nOutput: 15	EASY	[{"input": "42", "output": "42"}, {"input": "9", "output": "9"}]	["-1000 <= n <= 1000", "Use a variable to store the input before printing"]	[{"stdin": "42", "stdout": "42"}, {"stdin": "9", "stdout": "9"}, {"stdin": "-8", "stdout": "-8"}]	2026-07-27 10:45:33.471923	["Start Here"]
35	Whole Numbers Only	Read one whole number (an integer) and print it exactly as an integer.\n\nThe input is always an integer (no decimal point). Print it as an integer with no extra words or prompts.\n\nThis practices the integer data type: whole numbers like -2, -1, 0, 1, 2, …\n\nExample\nInput: 100\nOutput: 100	EASY	[{"input": "15", "output": "15"}, {"input": "0", "output": "0"}, {"input": "-99", "output": "-99"}]	["-10000 <= n <= 10000", "Treat the value as an integer"]	[{"stdin": "15", "stdout": "15"}, {"stdin": "0", "stdout": "0"}, {"stdin": "-99", "stdout": "-99"}]	2026-07-27 10:45:33.471923	["Start Here"]
36	Add the Pair	Read two integers from one line (separated by a space) and print their sum.\n\nInput format: a b (example: 2 3). Compute a + b and print a single integer — the sum only. Do not print the expression itself.\n\nThe + operator means add.\n\nExample\nInput: 2 3\nOutput: 5\n\nExample\nInput: -4 7\nOutput: 3	EASY	[{"input": "2 3", "output": "5"}, {"input": "-4 7", "output": "3"}, {"input": "10 -3", "output": "7"}]	["-1000 <= a, b <= 1000", "Two integers on one line, space-separated", "Print only the sum"]	[{"stdin": "2 3", "stdout": "5"}, {"stdin": "-4 7", "stdout": "3"}, {"stdin": "0 0", "stdout": "0"}, {"stdin": "10 -3", "stdout": "7"}]	2026-07-27 10:45:33.471923	["Start Here"]
37	Shop Checkout Math	Read three integers on one line: price, tax, and qty. Print the value of:\n\n(price + tax) * qty\n\nInput format: price tax qty (example: 10 2 3). Add price and tax first, then multiply by qty. Parentheses matter: (price + tax) * qty is not the same as price + tax * qty. Print one integer — the final total.\n\nExample\nInput: 10 2 3\nCalculation: (10 + 2) * 3 = 36\nOutput: 36	EASY	[{"input": "10 2 3", "output": "36"}, {"input": "5 0 4", "output": "20"}, {"input": "1 1 1", "output": "2"}]	["0 <= price, tax, qty <= 1000", "Use (price + tax) * qty"]	[{"stdin": "10 2 3", "stdout": "36"}, {"stdin": "5 0 4", "stdout": "20"}, {"stdin": "1 1 1", "stdout": "2"}, {"stdin": "8 2 0", "stdout": "0"}]	2026-07-27 10:45:33.471923	["Start Here"]
38	Notes for Future You	Read one integer (the score) and print double that score (score * 2).\n\nAlso add at least one comment in your source code that briefly explains what the program does. Comments are ignored by the computer and the judge — they never appear in the output.\n\nComment syntax:\nPython:  # this is a comment\nJava/C++: // this is a comment\n\nExample\nInput: 5\nOutput: 10	EASY	[{"input": "5", "output": "10"}, {"input": "0", "output": "0"}, {"input": "-3", "output": "-6"}]	["-1000 <= score <= 1000", "Print only score * 2", "Include at least one comment in your code"]	[{"stdin": "5", "stdout": "10"}, {"stdin": "0", "stdout": "0"}, {"stdin": "-3", "stdout": "-6"}, {"stdin": "100", "stdout": "200"}]	2026-07-27 10:45:33.471923	["Start Here"]
39	Catch the Wrong Operator	Read two integers a and b from one line (space-separated) and print their sum: a + b.\n\nCorrect result uses addition (+). A common bug is using multiplication (*) by mistake — that prints a product, not a sum. Print only the sum as one integer.\n\nCheck a sample: 3 + 4 should be 7. If you see 12, you multiplied instead of added.\n\nExample\nInput: 3 4\nOutput: 7	EASY	[{"input": "3 4", "output": "7"}, {"input": "10 0", "output": "10"}, {"input": "-2 5", "output": "3"}]	["-1000 <= a, b <= 1000", "Print a + b (not a * b)"]	[{"stdin": "3 4", "stdout": "7"}, {"stdin": "10 0", "stdout": "10"}, {"stdin": "-2 5", "stdout": "3"}, {"stdin": "6 6", "stdout": "12"}]	2026-07-27 10:45:33.471923	["Start Here"]
